package cajeta.net.reactor;

/**
 * **The async I/O reactor** — the internal engine that lets a fiber wait
 * for socket readiness *without blocking its carrier thread* (plan item
 * **NET-3.1**; see [`cajeta-docs/Net.md` §The reactor](../../../../../cajeta-docs/Net.md)).
 *
 * This type is **internal plumbing**, not an application surface. The
 * only public-facing methods on `cajeta.net` are the socket types'
 * `*Async` ops (NET-3.3); those lower their await points to the five
 * [`@Native`](#native-bridge) intrinsics bound here. Application code
 * never names `Reactor` directly — it `await`s a `Task` and the socket
 * op drives this engine underneath.
 *
 * ## What this is
 *
 * One reactor thread per process (v1) owns the OS event handle:
 *
 * | OS | Engine | Model |
 * |---|---|---|
 * | Linux | epoll (edge-triggered, `EPOLLONESHOT`) | readiness |
 * | macOS / BSD | kqueue (`EV_ONESHOT`) | readiness |
 * | Windows | IOCP (`WSARecv`/`AcceptEx`/`ConnectEx` + `GetQueuedCompletionStatus`) | completion |
 *
 * A fiber issuing an awaitable op (1) registers `(fd, interest,
 * fiber-handle)` with the reactor, (2) parks via the existing
 * `__cajeta_fiber` park path — control returns to the carrier, which
 * runs other fibers. The reactor blocks in `epoll_wait` / `kevent` /
 * `GetQueuedCompletionStatus`; on an event it moves the registered
 * fiber onto the carrier's ready deque (the same wake path
 * [`Channel`](../../threading/Channel.cajeta) and the timer wheel use).
 * The carrier resumes the fiber, which retries / finishes the syscall.
 * **No carrier ever blocks on socket I/O** — the invariant the whole
 * design protects.
 *
 * ## Native bridge contract
 *
 * The `@Native` declarations below bind the five NET-3.1 intrinsics in
 * [`__cajeta_net_reactor*`](../../../../native/cajeta_net_reactor.c) —
 * the same readiness vocabulary (`READ = 1`, `WRITE = 2`) the runtime's
 * R9.4 I/O reactor uses, so there is one interest contract runtime-wide:
 *
 *   - [`init`](#init) — lazy-create the OS event handle (idempotent).
 *   - [`register`](#register) / [`deregister`](#deregister) — arm / drop
 *     interest for the parking fiber (the cancellation path in NET-3.4
 *     calls `deregister`).
 *   - [`awaitReadable`](#awaitReadable) / [`awaitWritable`](#awaitWritable)
 *     — park the current fiber until the fd is ready, returning `true`
 *     on readiness.
 *
 * ## Implementation status (NET-3.1, increment 1)
 *
 * On **Linux** the await intrinsics delegate to the runtime's existing
 * epoll engine, so the fiber-park / carrier-wake integration is live
 * today. On **macOS / Windows** the dedicated kqueue / IOCP engines are
 * pending (NET-3.2/3.3); until they land the await path uses a portable
 * `select`-based readiness probe that is *correct* but blocks the
 * carrier — a bring-up fallback the dedicated engines replace without
 * any change to this surface or the five-intrinsic ABI.
 */
public final class Reactor {

    /** Interest bit: wait for the fd to become **readable**. Matches the
     *  native `CAJETA_IO_READ`. */
    static final int32 READ = 1;

    /** Interest bit: wait for the fd to become **writable**. Matches the
     *  native `CAJETA_IO_WRITE`. */
    static final int32 WRITE = 2;

    /** Probe result: the fd became ready. */
    static final int32 READY = 1;

    /** Probe result: the timeout elapsed before readiness. */
    static final int32 TIMEOUT = 0;

    /** Probe result: the readiness query failed (bad fd, etc.). */
    static final int32 ERROR = -1;

    // --- @Native bridges to the NET-3.1 reactor intrinsics --------------

    /**
     * Lazy-initialize the reactor engine (creates the OS event handle and,
     * where applicable, ensures Winsock is up). Idempotent. Returns `0` on
     * success, `-1` on failure.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_net_reactor_init")
    private static int32 initNative();

    /**
     * Arm `(fd, interest)` for the parking fiber `fiberHandle` (the opaque
     * runtime fiber pointer). Returns `0` on success. A no-op under the v1
     * one-shot model (the await intrinsic arms+parks atomically); present
     * so the ABI is complete for the NET-3.3 / NET-3.4 cancellation path.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_net_reactor_register")
    private static int32 registerNative(int32 fd, int32 interest,
                                        pointer fiberHandle);

    /**
     * Drop any armed interest for `fd`. Returns `0` on success. The
     * cancellation path (NET-3.4) calls this before raising the
     * cancellation sentinel so a timed-out op leaves no reactor leak.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_net_reactor_deregister")
    private static int32 deregisterNative(int32 fd);

    /**
     * The current live-registration balance — the number of
     * [`register`](#register) calls not yet matched by a
     * [`deregister`](#deregister). Zero when no fiber is parked on the
     * reactor. The NET-13.4 concurrency harness pins this to zero after a
     * settled wave of awaitable ops to prove the reactor leaks no
     * registrations on the completion *or* cancellation path.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_net_reactor_active_count")
    private static int32 activeCountNative();

    // --- NET-3.2 lifecycle bridges --------------------------------------

    /**
     * Has the net reactor been lazily initialized by a first awaitable op?
     * Returns `1` once [`init`](#init) has run, `0` before (and after a
     * [`shutdown`](#shutdown)). Lets a teardown path skip reactor work
     * entirely for a program that never did network I/O.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_net_reactor_started")
    private static int32 startedNative();

    /**
     * Clean, idempotent net-reactor teardown (NET-3.2): wake any
     * portable-path waiter via the shutdown pipe, drain the
     * live-registration balance to zero, reset the lazy-init latch, and
     * close the wake pipe. The runtime's `__cajeta_task_shutdown` calls the
     * underlying intrinsic automatically at carrier-pool teardown; this
     * bridge exposes it for an explicit, deterministic shutdown in tests.
     * Returns `0`.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_net_reactor_shutdown")
    private static int32 shutdownNative();

    /**
     * Park the current fiber until `fd` is readable. Returns `1`
     * ([`READY`](#READY)) on readiness, `-1` ([`ERROR`](#ERROR)) on
     * failure. On Linux this delegates to the runtime's epoll fiber-park
     * engine (the carrier runs other fibers while parked).
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_net_await_readable")
    private static int32 awaitReadableNative(int32 fd);

    /** Park the current fiber until `fd` is writable. See
     *  [`awaitReadableNative`](#awaitReadableNative). */
    @Native("__cajeta_net_await_writable")
    private static int32 awaitWritableNative(int32 fd);

    /**
     * Portable single-fd readiness probe: block the calling thread until
     * `fd` is ready for any bit in `interest`, or `timeoutMs` elapses
     * (negative ⇒ block indefinitely). Returns [`READY`](#READY) /
     * [`TIMEOUT`](#TIMEOUT) / [`ERROR`](#ERROR). This is the deterministic
     * `select`-based body the native golden-vector tests pin and the
     * non-fiber fallback the await path uses.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_net_reactor_poll_fd")
    private static int32 pollFdNative(int32 fd, int32 interest,
                                      int32 timeoutMs);

    // --- internal reactor surface (NET-3.3 socket ops call these) -------

    /**
     * Ensure the reactor engine is initialized. Idempotent; safe to call
     * before every await. Returns `true` on success.
     */
    static boolean init() {
        return Reactor.initNative() == 0;
    }

    /**
     * Arm reactor interest for `fd` on behalf of the **current** parking
     * fiber, bumping the live-registration balance ([`activeCount`](#activeCount))
     * by one. The cajeta-side companion of
     * [`registerNative`](#registerNative): the `fiberHandle` the persistent
     * (kqueue/IOCP) engines will key their waiter table on is the *current*
     * fiber, which under the v1 one-shot model the native layer derives
     * itself — so this surface passes `null` and the counter is what the
     * leak-invariant (NET-11.4 / NET-13.4) keys on. Always paired with a
     * matching [`deregister`](#deregister) on **every** exit path (the
     * [`awaitReadable`](#awaitReadable) / [`awaitWritable`](#awaitWritable)
     * adapters bracket the park in `try`/`finally` to guarantee that).
     *
     * @param fd       the socket descriptor being awaited.
     * @param interest [`READ`](#READ) and/or [`WRITE`](#WRITE).
     * @return `true` on success.
     */
    static boolean register(int32 fd, int32 interest) {
        // Typed-null pointer local rather than a bare `null` arg: the @Native
        // forwarder builds the call against `registerNative`'s declared
        // `pointer` param, and a bare `null` literal in arg position can be
        // typed as an object-ref null (mismatching the raw pointer the C
        // `void* fiber_handle` expects). Binding it to a `pointer`-typed local
        // pins the type. v1 ignores the handle (one-shot model), so null is
        // the correct value.
        pointer noHandle = null;
        return Reactor.registerNative(fd, interest, noHandle) == 0;
    }

    /**
     * Park the current fiber until `fd` is readable, **bracketed by a
     * reactor registration** that is dropped on *every* exit path — the
     * NET-11.4 cancellation/deadline adapter.
     *
     * The body the NET-3.3 `readAsync` / `acceptAsync` / `recvFromAsync`
     * readiness loop calls after a non-blocking syscall reports
     * `WouldBlock`. It [`register`](#register)s `(fd, READ)` so the
     * in-flight op is visible in [`activeCount`](#activeCount), then parks.
     * The `finally` [`deregister`](#deregister)s **whether the park returns
     * readable, returns an error, *or* the wait is abandoned because the
     * enclosing `Tasks.withTimeout` / `withDeadline` fired the fiber's
     * `cancel_with` and the runtime threw the cancellation sentinel through
     * this frame** (see [`cajeta-docs/Net.md` §The async model](../../../../../cajeta-docs/Net.md)).
     * That is the *deregister-on-cancel* contract every reactor op honors:
     * a cancelled or timed-out await leaves the live-registration balance
     * back at the value it had on entry — never a leak.
     *
     * @param fd the socket descriptor to wait on.
     * @return `true` when the fd became readable, `false` on a reactor
     *         error.
     */
    static boolean awaitReadable(int32 fd) {
        Reactor.register(fd, Reactor.READ);
        boolean ready = false;
        try {
            ready = Reactor.awaitReadableNative(fd) == Reactor.READY;
        } finally {
            Reactor.deregister(fd);
        }
        return ready;
    }

    /**
     * Park the current fiber until `fd` is writable, **bracketed by a
     * reactor registration** dropped on every exit path — the writable
     * twin of [`awaitReadable`](#awaitReadable) and the same NET-11.4
     * cancellation/deadline adapter.
     *
     * The body the NET-3.3 `connectAsync` / `writeAsync` / `sendToAsync`
     * readiness loop calls after a non-blocking syscall reports
     * `WouldBlock` / `in-progress`. As with the readable form, the
     * `finally` [`deregister`](#deregister)s on the normal-completion,
     * error, **and** cancellation-sentinel paths, so a cancelled or
     * timed-out write leaves no reactor registration behind.
     *
     * @param fd the socket descriptor to wait on.
     * @return `true` when the fd became writable, `false` on a reactor
     *         error.
     */
    static boolean awaitWritable(int32 fd) {
        Reactor.register(fd, Reactor.WRITE);
        boolean ready = false;
        try {
            ready = Reactor.awaitWritableNative(fd) == Reactor.READY;
        } finally {
            Reactor.deregister(fd);
        }
        return ready;
    }

    // --- NET-3.4 deadline-aware await --------------------------------------
    //
    // The untimed [`awaitReadable`](#awaitReadable) / [`awaitWritable`](#awaitWritable)
    // adapters above deregister on **completion**, **error**, and the
    // **cancellation-sentinel** unwind (an enclosing `Tasks.withTimeout` firing
    // the fiber's `cancel_with` — see [`cajeta.threading.Tasks`](../../threading/Tasks.cajeta)).
    // That is the canonical deadline composition the spec names
    // ([`cajeta-docs/Net.md` §The async model](../../../../../cajeta-docs/Net.md)):
    // `Tasks.withTimeout(d, spawn conn.readAsync(buf))` cancels the parked fiber,
    // the sentinel unwinds through `awaitReadable`'s `finally`, and the
    // registration is dropped — *deregister-on-cancel*, the contract every
    // reactor op honors.
    //
    // The `*Timed` siblings below add the **self-contained** deadline form: a
    // single await that bounds its own wait by `timeoutMs` (via the portable
    // [`pollFd`](#pollFd) probe) and **deregisters on the timeout path too**, not
    // only on cancellation. The same register→wait→`finally`-deregister bracket,
    // so a deadline that elapses with the fd still idle leaves the
    // live-registration balance ([`activeCount`](#activeCount)) back at its entry
    // value — never a leak (the `timedReadDeregistersOnTimeout` acceptance,
    // NET-3.4). They return the tri-state [`READY`](#READY) / [`TIMEOUT`](#TIMEOUT)
    // / [`ERROR`](#ERROR) so the socket-level timed op can map a `TIMEOUT` to a
    // [`TimedOutException`](../TimedOutException.cajeta) and a `READY` to a retry,
    // distinguishing "the deadline fired" from "the fd is ready" without an
    // out-of-band signal. A negative `timeoutMs` is "no deadline" and degenerates
    // to the untimed wait's semantics (but still through the probe, so it is the
    // off-fiber-usable form).

    /**
     * The current time in **milliseconds** (same clock as
     * `Cajeta.currentTimeNanos()`, scaled down). The unit the NET-3.4
     * deadline-bounded socket ops budget in: a caller computes a deadline as
     * `Reactor.nowMillis() + timeoutMs` once, then passes the *remaining* budget
     * (`deadline - Reactor.nowMillis()`) to [`awaitReadableTimed`](#awaitReadableTimed)
     * each spin so a multi-wait op (short read, retry, short read) cannot reset
     * its own deadline. Kept here so the deadline arithmetic lives next to the
     * adapters that consume it.
     *
     * @return milliseconds on the runtime clock (`int64` so it does not wrap for
     *         any practical deadline).
     */
    static int64 nowMillis() {
        return Cajeta.currentTimeNanos() / 1000000;
    }

    /**
     * Park (off a fiber: block the calling thread) until `fd` is readable **or**
     * `timeoutMs` milliseconds elapse, **bracketed by a reactor registration
     * dropped on every exit path** — the NET-3.4 deadline-aware twin of
     * [`awaitReadable`](#awaitReadable).
     *
     * Unlike [`awaitReadable`](#awaitReadable) (which waits without a bound and
     * relies on an enclosing `Tasks.withTimeout` cancellation to abandon the
     * wait), this form bounds the wait itself: it [`register`](#register)s
     * `(fd, READ)`, runs the portable [`pollFd`](#pollFd) probe with the deadline,
     * and the `finally` [`deregister`](#deregister)s on the readable, timeout,
     * error, **and** cancellation-sentinel paths alike. So a deadline that
     * elapses with the socket still idle returns [`TIMEOUT`](#TIMEOUT) *and*
     * leaves no reactor registration behind — the `timedReadDeregistersOnTimeout`
     * acceptance.
     *
     * @param fd        the socket descriptor to wait on.
     * @param timeoutMs the deadline in milliseconds; negative ⇒ no deadline.
     * @return [`READY`](#READY) when the fd became readable, [`TIMEOUT`](#TIMEOUT)
     *         when the deadline elapsed first, [`ERROR`](#ERROR) on a probe
     *         failure.
     */
    static int32 awaitReadableTimed(int32 fd, int32 timeoutMs) {
        Reactor.register(fd, Reactor.READ);
        int32 r = Reactor.ERROR;
        try {
            r = Reactor.pollFdNative(fd, Reactor.READ, timeoutMs);
        } finally {
            Reactor.deregister(fd);
        }
        return r;
    }

    /**
     * Park (off a fiber: block the calling thread) until `fd` is writable **or**
     * `timeoutMs` milliseconds elapse, bracketed by a reactor registration
     * dropped on every exit path — the writable twin of
     * [`awaitReadableTimed`](#awaitReadableTimed) and the same NET-3.4
     * deregister-on-timeout bracket.
     *
     * @param fd        the socket descriptor to wait on.
     * @param timeoutMs the deadline in milliseconds; negative ⇒ no deadline.
     * @return [`READY`](#READY) when the fd became writable, [`TIMEOUT`](#TIMEOUT)
     *         when the deadline elapsed first, [`ERROR`](#ERROR) on a probe
     *         failure.
     */
    static int32 awaitWritableTimed(int32 fd, int32 timeoutMs) {
        Reactor.register(fd, Reactor.WRITE);
        int32 r = Reactor.ERROR;
        try {
            r = Reactor.pollFdNative(fd, Reactor.WRITE, timeoutMs);
        } finally {
            Reactor.deregister(fd);
        }
        return r;
    }

    /**
     * Drop any reactor interest armed for `fd`. The cancellation /
     * cleanup hook (NET-3.4) that guarantees a cancelled or timed-out op
     * leaves no reactor registration behind.
     *
     * @param fd the socket descriptor to disarm.
     * @return `true` on success.
     */
    static boolean deregister(int32 fd) {
        return Reactor.deregisterNative(fd) == 0;
    }

    /**
     * The current number of live reactor registrations — `register` calls
     * not yet balanced by a `deregister`. Zero when nothing is parked on the
     * reactor. The leak-check signal the NET-13.4 harness asserts returns to
     * zero after a wave of awaitable ops settles.
     *
     * @return the live-registration count (never negative).
     */
    static int32 activeCount() {
        return Reactor.activeCountNative();
    }

    /**
     * Whether the net reactor has been lazily initialized by a first
     * awaitable op (NET-3.2). `false` before the first await and after a
     * [`shutdown`](#shutdown). A teardown path can skip reactor work when
     * this is `false` — a program that never did network I/O pays nothing.
     *
     * @return `true` once the reactor has initialized.
     */
    static boolean started() {
        return Reactor.startedNative() == 1;
    }

    /**
     * Clean, idempotent net-reactor shutdown (NET-3.2): wake any
     * portable-path waiter, drain the live-registration balance to zero,
     * reset the lazy-init latch, and close the shutdown wake pipe.
     *
     * The runtime invokes the underlying intrinsic from
     * `__cajeta_task_shutdown` at carrier-pool teardown, so application code
     * normally never calls this; it is exposed for an explicit,
     * deterministic shutdown (and the lifecycle tests). After it returns,
     * [`started`](#started) is `false` and [`activeCount`](#activeCount) is
     * zero. Safe to call repeatedly and when the reactor never started.
     *
     * @return `true` (the teardown has nothing actionable to fail on).
     */
    static boolean shutdown() {
        return Reactor.shutdownNative() == 0;
    }

    /**
     * Wait — blocking the calling thread — until `fd` is ready for
     * `interest`, or `timeoutMs` elapses (negative ⇒ no timeout).
     *
     * The portable bring-up primitive: usable off a fiber (where parking
     * has no meaning) and the deterministic surface the native tests
     * pin. Prefer [`awaitReadable`](#awaitReadable) /
     * [`awaitWritable`](#awaitWritable) on a fiber so the carrier stays
     * free.
     *
     * @param fd        the socket descriptor to probe.
     * @param interest  [`READ`](#READ) and/or [`WRITE`](#WRITE).
     * @param timeoutMs milliseconds to wait, or negative for no limit.
     * @return [`READY`](#READY), [`TIMEOUT`](#TIMEOUT), or
     *         [`ERROR`](#ERROR).
     */
    static int32 pollFd(int32 fd, int32 interest, int32 timeoutMs) {
        return Reactor.pollFdNative(fd, interest, timeoutMs);
    }
}
